---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import IdealWeightCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/IdealWeightCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ideal-weight-calculator">
  <IdealWeightCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose metric or imperial and enter your height. Height is the only required input.</li>
      <li>
        Add your current weight to see your distance from each estimate and your BMI.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pick a frame size to apply the Hamwi ±10 percent allowance. If you are not sure, leave it on
        medium.
      </li>
      <li>
        Compare all four formulas with the healthy BMI band on the single scale. Note the spread between
        them.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The four formulas</h2>
    <p>
      All four ideal weight formulas have the same structure: a base weight at 5 feet (152.4 cm), plus a
      fixed amount for each inch above that. Only the base and the slope differ.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Formula</th>
            <th scope="col">Year</th>
            <th scope="col">Men</th>
            <th scope="col">Women</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Hamwi</td>
            <td>1964</td>
            <td>48.0 kg + 2.7 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
            <td>45.5 kg + 2.2 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Devine</td>
            <td>1974</td>
            <td>50.0 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
            <td>45.5 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Robinson</td>
            <td>1983</td>
            <td>52.0 kg + 1.9 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
            <td>49.0 kg + 1.7 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Miller</td>
            <td>1983</td>
            <td>56.2 kg + 1.41 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
            <td>53.1 kg + 1.36 kg per inch over 5 ft</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The slopes differ, so the formulas cross over. Miller has the highest base and the shallowest
      slope, so it gives the highest result for short people and the lowest for tall people. Hamwi is
      the opposite. At about 5 ft 8 in, all four are within a few kilograms of each other.
    </p>

    <h2>Where these formulas came from, and what they are for</h2>
    <p>
      None of them was made to give a personal goal. B. J. Devine published his in 1974 to standardise
      gentamicin dosing. An antibiotic dose based on total body weight is too high for people with a lot
      of fat, because fat has poor blood flow and holds little of a water-soluble drug. Robinson and
      Miller later fitted the same Metropolitan Life insurance data again. Hamwi's rule came from the
      1964 American Diabetes Association guidance for diabetes meal planning.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ideal body weight is still used every day in clinical practice, for:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Weight-based drug dosing, particularly aminoglycoside antibiotics and chemotherapy</li>
      <li>Setting tidal volumes in mechanical ventilation (6–8 ml per kg of predicted body weight)</li>
      <li>Estimating baseline nutritional requirements in hospital</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      They are not a target. They contain no information about muscle, frame or body fat, and the
      insurance data came from mid-20th-century American populations.
    </p>

    <h2>Ideal weight at common heights</h2>
    <p>Average of the four formulas, alongside the healthy BMI range for comparison.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Height</th>
            <th scope="col">Men (average of 4)</th>
            <th scope="col">Women (average of 4)</th>
            <th scope="col">Healthy BMI range</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1.55 m · 5 ft 1 in</td><td>53.5 kg</td><td>48.7 kg</td><td>44 – 60 kg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.60 m · 5 ft 3 in</td><td>57.3 kg</td><td>52.2 kg</td><td>47 – 64 kg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.65 m · 5 ft 5 in</td><td>61.1 kg</td><td>55.8 kg</td><td>50 – 68 kg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.70 m · 5 ft 7 in</td><td>65.0 kg</td><td>59.3 kg</td><td>53 – 72 kg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.75 m · 5 ft 9 in</td><td>68.8 kg</td><td>62.8 kg</td><td>57 – 76 kg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.80 m · 5 ft 11 in</td><td>72.6 kg</td><td>66.4 kg</td><td>60 – 81 kg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.85 m · 6 ft 1 in</td><td>76.5 kg</td><td>69.9 kg</td><td>63 – 85 kg</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1.90 m · 6 ft 3 in</td><td>80.3 kg</td><td>73.5 kg</td><td>67 – 90 kg</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The formulas are close together, usually within 4 kg, and they are always in the lower half of the
      healthy BMI range. That comes from their insurance-table origins. It does not show that the upper
      half of the BMI range is unhealthy.
    </p>

    <h2>Frame size and why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      Skeletal frame varies. Two people of the same height can differ by several kilograms of bone and
      connective tissue, before muscle or fat. Hamwi's original rule allowed ±10 percent for large and
      small frames, and this calculator applies it.
    </p>
    <p>Two quick ways to estimate your frame:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Wrist test: put your thumb and middle finger around the opposite wrist. If they overlap, your
        frame is small. If they just touch, it is medium. If they do not meet, it is large.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wrist circumference against height: for a woman under 1.63 m, under 14 cm is
        small and over 16.5 cm is large. For a man over 1.65 m, under 16.5 cm is small and over 19 cm
        is large.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A more useful way to think about it</h2>
    <p>
      There is no single ideal weight, and to chase one usually does harm. The evidence supports a
      healthy range, plus a few markers that matter much more than the number on the scale:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A waist under half your height</li>
      <li>Body fat inside the fitness or athletic bands for your sex</li>
      <li>Enough muscle to do what you want to do, now and at seventy</li>
      <li>Blood pressure, lipids and fasting glucose in range</li>
      <li>A weight you can hold without constant restriction</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A person at the top of the healthy BMI range who lifts and walks daily is in better shape than a
      person who reaches a Devine number through chronic under-eating. Use the range above as a check,
      then judge yourself on the things that respond to training and habits.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
